# LinguaForge project rules

## Curriculum and content

- Treat `apps/web/src/content/curriculum.ts` and `apps/web/src/content/lessons-ampliadas.ts` as the lesson source of truth. Generate static pages with `scripts/generate-published-lesson-html.mjs`; never hand-edit generated output.
- Keep all published lessons complete in `es`, `pt-BR`, and `ca`. A lesson is `publicada` only when its objectives, theory, starter code, verification tests, hints, and voice introduction are reviewed in all three locales.
- Preserve the distinction between a published lesson and a roadmap item. Do not claim CEFR/MCER certification, learner outcomes, or course completion without evidence.
- Maintain at least 200 distinct HTML learning pages. Current contract: 17 published lessons × 3 locales × 4 sections = 204 pages. The CI test must validate the generated files, unique documents, and local links.

## HTML and accessibility

- Escape all curriculum text with the generator's `escapeHtml` before inserting it into HTML. Keep generated pages script-free and use local, relative links.
- Each learning page must declare its locale, viewport, one primary heading, a skip link, and a clear route back to the catalogue and interactive workspace.
- Keep keyboard navigation, visible focus, screen-reader names, sufficient contrast, and meaning that does not depend on color. Verify changes against [Microsoft Edge's accessible website design guidance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/accessibility/design).

## Runtime, security, and tests

- Preserve the existing isolation boundary for learner-submitted code. Never execute learner input in the server process or widen a tool's network or filesystem access without a reviewed threat model.
- Preserve content attribution and provenance. Do not silently import third-party text, audio, models, or datasets under the code's MIT license.
- Before changing the Next.js app, read the relevant installed guidance under `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` as required by `apps/web/AGENTS.md`.
- Run the `apps/web` typecheck, lint, Vitest suite, and production build. The suite's static-library test is the acceptance gate for the 204-page contract.
- Keep GitHub Actions permissions read-only unless an individual job requires a documented write permission. Pin actions to full commit SHAs, following [GitHub's secure-use guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use).

## Cursor rule format

- Keep this file in `.cursor/rules` as a version-controlled `.mdc` project rule, following [Cursor's project-rules format](https://prod.cursor.com/docs/rules).
